  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Decent work effectively mainstreamed in the agenda and programmes of regional and sub regional partner institutions, including the AU UN, and the AfDB</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Significant progress has been achieved in strengthening partnerships and integrating decent work priorities into regional frameworks, including the systematic promotion of gender equality. Building on these achievements, this regional programme aims to further enhance policy coherence, coordination and impact on advancing employment, social justice and inclusive development in line with Agenda 2030 and Agenda 2063.
Drawing on lessons from the 2024&#x2013;2025 biennium, the Regional Programme Outcome contributes to the result of strengthened capacity of African constituents and regional institutions to design and implement coordinated, inclusive and gender-responsive policies that support a renewed social contract. It supports African constituents in shaping regional priorities, including through the 15th African Regional Meeting, and strengthens engagement with key partners such as the African Union Commission, the Economic Commission for Africa and the African Development Bank.
Through an integrated approach, the programme delivers concrete results, including the implementation of AU/ILO Joint Programme initiatives and joint actions with regional partners to promote decent job creation. It also strengthens the capacity of Africa Government Group members of the ILO Governing Body to engage in key policy areas, including decent work in the platform economy and priorities of the International Labour Conference. In addition, it supports the dissemination and use of knowledge tools, such as the Model Law and Toolkit, and promotes increased uptake of international labour standards, including Convention No. 190, and the validation of the Africa Minimum Wage Report.
The programme benefits policymakers, parliamentarians, labour institutions, and employers&#x2019; and workers&#x2019; organizations. By enhancing partnerships, capacities and policy coherence, it contributes to more effective and inclusive labour market outcomes and sustainable development across the region.</narrative>
  </description>
